{"study_accession":"PRJNA352224","secondary_study_accession":"SRP092457","title":"Harnessing RNA sequencing for global, unbiased evaluation of two new adjuvants for dendritic-cell immunotherapy","description":"Effective stimulation of immune cells is crucial for the success of cancer immunotherapies. Current approaches to evaluate the efficiency of stimuli activating immune cells are mainly defined by known flow cytometry-based cell activation or cell maturation markers. This method however does not give a complete overview of the achieved activation state and may leave important side effects unnoticed. Here, we used an unbiased RNA sequencing (RNA-seq)-based approach to compare the capacity of four clinical-grade dendritic cell (DC) activation stimuli used to prepare DC-vaccines composed of various types of DC subsets; the already clinically applied GM-CSF and Frühsommer meningoencephalitis (FSME) prophylactic vaccine and the novel clinical grade adjuvants protamine-RNA (pRNA) complexes and CpG-P. We found that GM-CSF and pRNA have similar effects on their target cells, whereas pRNA and CpG-P induce stronger tType I interferon (IFN) expression than FSME. In general, the pathways most affected by all stimuli were related to immune activity and cell migration. GM-CSF stimulation, however, also induced a significant increase of genes related to nonsense-mediated decay, indicating a possible deleterious effect of this stimulus. Taken together, all novel stimuli appear to be promising alternatives. Our study demonstrates how RNA-seq based investigation of changes in a large number of genes and gene groups can be exploited for fast and unbiased, global evaluation of clinical-grade stimuli, as opposed to the general limited evaluation of a pre-specified set of genes, by which one might miss important biological effects detrimental for vaccine efficacy. Overall design: mRNA profiles of CD1c+ mDCs and pDCs, MACS isolated from 3 healthy volunteers and stimulated with 4 clinical grade stimuli, were generated by deep RNA sequencing using HiSeq 2000 System (TruSeq SBS KIT-HS V3，Illumina)","pubmed":"28186996;30455688;33675038","geo_series":"","n_samples":23,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN05968542","study_accession":"PRJNA352224","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"pDC CpGP_donor 3","description":"pDC CpGP_donor 3","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2372288","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2017-01-04","attributes":[["source_name","Blood"],["health condition","Healthy Volunteer"],["tissue","Blood"],["isolation method","MACS"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR4841406","experiment_accession":"SRX2321318","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2000","read_count":26520448,"base_count":4773680640,"first_public":"2017-01-04","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2000 sequencing: GSM2372288: pDC CpGP_donor 3 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN05968543","study_accession":"PRJNA352224","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"pDC FSME_donor 2","description":"pDC FSME_donor 2","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2372282","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2017-01-04","attributes":[["source_name","Blood"],["health condition","Healthy Volunteer"],["tissue","Blood"],["isolation method","MACS"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR4841400","experiment_accession":"SRX2321312","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2000","read_count":26477821,"base_count":4766007780,"first_public":"2017-01-04","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2000 sequencing: GSM2372282: pDC FSME_donor 2 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN05968544","study_accession":"PRJNA352224","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"pDC FSME_donor 3","description":"pDC FSME_donor 3","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2372283","access":"open","dbgap":